/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A signal is periodic when x(t) = x(t + T₀) for every t, where T₀ is the smallest positive value that works: the period, in seconds.
   - Fourier's claim is that x(t) equals a sum of these harmonics.
   - ∫T₀ cos(nω₀t)cos(mω₀t)dt = T₀/2 if n = m ≥ 1, T₀ if n = m = 0, and 0 if n ≠ m
   - Here n and m are harmonic numbers; ω₀ = 2π/T₀ rad/s; the integral runs over one period of T₀ seconds.
   - This is orthogonality: different harmonics do not overlap.
   - x(t) = a₀/2 + Σn=1∞ [aₙcos(nω₀t) + bₙsin(nω₀t)]
   - Here x(t) is the periodic signal with period T₀ seconds; ω₀ = 2π/T₀ rad/s; aₙ and bₙ are real coefficients in the units of x(t); a₀/2 is the average or DC value.
   - aₙ = (2/T₀)∫T₀ x(t)cos(nω₀t)dt, bₙ = (2/T₀)∫T₀ x(t)sin(nω₀t)dt, a₀ = (2/T₀)∫T₀ x(t)dt
   - Here the factor 2/T₀ cancels the T₀/2 from orthogonality; all three integrals run over one period.
   - x(t) = Σn=−∞∞ cₙejnω₀t, cₙ = (1/T₀)∫T₀ x(t)e−jnω₀tdt
*/
