/* book exam traps: Dropping the 1/T₀ in front of the cₙ integral, or using 2/T₀ for cₙ because the trigonometric form uses 2/T₀ for aₙ. Dropping 1/T₀ multiplies every cₙ by T₀; using 2/T₀ instead of 1/T₀ doubles every cₙ. | Writing ω₀ = 1/T₀. The fundamental frequency is f₀ = 1/T₀ hertz, but the angular frequency is ω₀ = 2π/T₀ rad/s, and every harmonic multiple must use ω₀. | Mixing the two DC conventions. With x(t) = a₀/2 + Σ… and a₀ = (2/T₀)∫x(t)dt, as in this book, the DC value is a₀/2 = c₀. Some books write x(t) = a₀ + Σ… with a₀ = (1/T₀)∫x(t)dt, and then a₀ itself is the DC value. Pick one convention and keep it through the an | Summing the exponential series only from n = 1. The sum runs from −∞ to ∞, and for real signals c−n = cₙ*, so the cosine amplitude is 2|cₙ|, not |cₙ|. | Getting the sign wrong in a time shift: x(t − t₀) multiplies cₙ by e−jnω₀t₀. A delay gives a negative phase; e+jnω₀t₀ is a time advance. */
