/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Start with a periodic signal xT₀(t), period T₀.
   - X(ω) = ∫−∞∞ x(t)e−jωtdt
   - Here x(t) is the time signal (V if voltage); ω is angular frequency in rad/s; j=√−1; X(ω) is the complex spectrum.
   - x(t) = (1/2π)∫−∞∞ X(ω)ejωtdω
   - Here X(ω) is the spectrum and dω is a small step of angular frequency.
   - The transform exists when x(t) meets the Dirichlet conditions: ∫|x(t)|dt<∞, and only finitely many maxima, minima and finite jumps in any finite interval.
   - Use Sa(y)=sin y/y.
   - A periodic signal is not aperiodic, but its transform is still written as impulse trains: x(t)=Σ cn ejnω₀t ↔ 2πΣ cn δ(ω−nω₀).
   - A signal is time-limited if x(t)=0 outside a finite interval.
*/
